L18 is the Team row for QAB % under Last 5 Games. C18 pulls the team value from the last game's sheet (='Azalia Park 7-2-12'!I18), so there are two plausible completions: an average of the player rows, =AVERAGE(L5:L17), or the cross-sheet pattern used in L5:L17 extended to row 18, =AVERAGEA('Delany Park 6-26'!I18+...)/4.

Row 18 otherwise uses simple aggregates over the column (K18 is =SUM(K5:K17)), and for a percentage column the matching aggregate is an average, so I chose that one.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(L5:L17)"}

Column C is "Ave Score", so the formula should average the Film score column (AI) for the director in column A, following the same pattern as column E (Ave Time).

The actual row ranges are hidden by the "@" placeholders, so I assumed whole-column references.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF(Film!E:E,\"*\"&A2&\"*\",Film!AI:AI)"}

L2 most likely mirrors L4 for the home team: count the wins in the team's last K2 home games. The L4 formula counts away games for the team in J4 where F equals 1, starting from the K4-th most recent away row. Note that F=1 actually means a home win, so L4 arguably should use 2 for away wins; for the home row, 1 is the correct value.

So I swapped column C for column B and row 4 references for row 2, keeping F=1.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS(INDEX($B:$B,AGGREGATE(14,6,ROW($B$6:$B$135)/($B$6:$B$135=$J2),$K2)):$B$135,$J2,INDEX($F:$F,AGGREGATE(14,6,ROW($B$6:$B$135)/($B$6:$B$135=$J2),$K2)):$F$135,1)"}

Column P is DELTA, sitting between D1 (column N) and GAMMA (column Q), so the formula is most likely the Black-Scholes call delta with a dividend yield: exp(-q*t)*N(d1).

Here O is the dividend yield, M is time in years, and N is d1. The existing GAMMA formula uses the same EXP(-1*M2*O2) term, so I matched that style.

{"formula": "=EXP(-1*M2*O2)*NORMSDIST(N2)"}

Loan 2 layout: B15 is the loan amount, B16 the rate, B22 the number of payments and B23 the balloon payment. B25 is the regular payment under balloon definition 1, where the balloon is the final balance left after the last regular payment. That makes the balloon the future value in PMT:

PMT(B16/12,B22,-B15,B23), rounded up to match the pattern used in B9.

{"formula": "=ROUNDUP(PMT(B16/12,B22,-B15,B23),2)"}

W4 is under the "SE (BP)" header, and the neighbouring column X (SE for CP) uses the pattern `=SUMSQ(V@:V@)/B@`, which is most likely a running sum of squared errors, `=SUMSQ(V$4:V4)/B4`. The MAD columns follow the same shape: Y uses `SUM(U:U)/B` for BP and Z uses `SUM(V:V)/B` for CP.

By analogy, the BP version of SE should use the BP absolute error column U, anchored at row 4 so it can be filled down.

{"formula": "=SUMSQ(U$4:U4)/B4"}

Looking at the pattern from the sibling blocks: DG11 and DT11 (the "Exceeding Expectation – Lower" columns of the 2nd and 3rd assessment blocks) use `=IF(CU@="","",CU@-CS@)`. CT11 is the same column in the 1st assessment block, where CU is Upper (the max score) and CS is the interval.

Note that the DG/DT formulas appear to reference CU/CS at the same row, so the same formula fits CT11 directly.

{"formula": "=IF(CU11=\"\",\"\",CU11-CS11)"}

Row 1 has carrier names in A–E and "Lowest Carrier 200" in F. Cell F3 most likely finds the carrier with the lowest price in that row. Row 2 holds numbers (probably weights), and D3:D15 seems empty for Old D, though MIN ignores blanks.

The likely formula looks up the lowest price in A3:E3 and returns the matching carrier name from the header row, with absolute row references on the header so it can be filled down.

{"formula": "=INDEX($A$1:$E$1,MATCH(MIN(A3:E3),A3:E3,0))"}

B12 is labelled "Weekday" and B13 looks up B12 in the G4:H10 table, so B12 should return the weekday number of the date in B11 (Today plus the modulus).

The lookup table probably numbers Monday to Sunday as 1 to 7, which would call for `WEEKDAY(B11,2)`. I can't see the values in G4:G10, though, so I've kept the simpler default form; add the `,2` argument if Monday is 1 in that table.

{"formula": "=WEEKDAY(B11)"}
